    "        cnn_model_1.count_params(),\n",
    "        cnn_model_2.count_params(),\n",
    "    ]\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "final_results['Test Accuracy'] = final_results['Test Accuracy'] * 100\n",
    "final_results = final_results.sort_values(by='Test Accuracy', ascending=False).set_index('Model')\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"=== Final Model Performance Comparison (Trained from Scratch) ===\")\n",
    "print(final_results.to_string(formatters={'Test Accuracy': '{:.2f}%'.format, 'Parameters': '{:,}'.format}))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Questions for Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "1.  **MLP vs. CNN**: Compare the performance of the Baseline MLP to the Small CNN. The CNN has far fewer parameters but performs much better. Why is the CNN architecture so much more effective for image data than a standard MLP?\n",
    "\n",
    "2.  **Architectural Improvements**: Compare the Small CNN and the Advanced CNN. How did adding depth, Batch Normalization, and Dropout impact the final test accuracy and the training process? Look at the training/validation plots for clues.\n",
    "\n",
    "3.  **ResNet vs. MobileNet**: Look at the predictions from the ResNet50 and MobileNetV2 models on your own images. How do their top predictions compare? What does this tell you about the trade-off between a model's size/complexity (ResNet is much larger) and its efficiency (MobileNet is designed for speed)?\n",
    "\n",
    "4.  **Strategic Implications**: Imagine you are building a system to automatically tag photos for a stock image website. Based on the performance jump from MLP to CNN and the power of pre-trained models, what is the strategic value of choosing the right model architecture for a specific type of data (like images)?"
